{
  "rules": [
    "Regime/level bias: when the last 10–20 days show a clear downtrend or rollover, the model stays anchored to the prior high regime and is systematically too high (often by ~5–15 €/t). Apply a downward level shift in declining regimes.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors grow with horizon. Near-term (days 1–5) is close, but beyond ~day 10 the model increasingly overpredicts and fails to follow the realized sell-off. Use a horizon-based negative adjustment that increases with lead time.",
    "Missed mean reversion after peaks: after a local top/spike (late-history highs), the model projects persistence/renewed gains, while reality mean-reverts lower. If recent history contains a sharp run-up, damp the forecast slope and pull the path back toward a lower mean.",
    "Trend-lag: the forecast path reacts too slowly to turning points (rollover from uptrend to downtrend). When momentum over the last ~5–10 days turns negative, force an earlier inflection in the forecast (reduce levels and impose negative drift).",
    "Volatility asymmetry: the model underestimates downside magnitude during drawdowns (predicts mild declines/sideways while outcomes drop sharply). In negative-momentum regimes, widen downside moves (increase absolute daily declines) rather than keeping a flat/high plateau."
  ]
}
